      "source": [
        "We can describe this relationship as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{Number of operations} \\propto n$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $n$ represents the number of elements in the input.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key idea:** When an algorithm processes every element once, its *time complexity* is generally linear."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Big O notation: Describing how algorithms scale\n",
        "\n",
        "We use **Big O notation** to describe how computational work grows as the input size increases.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this seminar, we will concentrate on four important complexity classes.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Complexity | Name | Example algorithm|\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| $O(1)$ | Constant | Accessing an element at a known list index |\n",
        "| $O(\\log n)$ | Logarithmic | Binary search |\n",
        "| $O(n)$ | Linear | Linear search |\n",
        "| $O(n^2)$ | Quadratic | Selection sort; insertion sort in its worst case |\n",
        "\n",
        "### Understanding the difference\n",
        "\n",
        "The following table gives illustrative operation counts. \n",
        "\n",
        "These are not exact predictions for every algorithm.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Input size $n$ | $O(1)$ | $O(\\log_2 n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n^2)$ |\n",
        "|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| 10 | 1 | 4 | 10 | 100 |\n",
        "| 100 | 1 | 7 | 100 | 10,000 |\n",
        "| 1,000 | 1 | 10 | 1,000 | 1,000,000 |\n",
        "| 10,000 | 1 | 14 | 10,000 | 100,000,000 |\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice what happens when the input size increases from 1,000 to 10,000:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $O(1)$: no increase.\n",
        "- $O(\\log n)$: increases by only a few operations.\n",
        "- $O(n)$: increases tenfold.\n",
        "- $O(n^2)$: increases one hundredfold.\n",
        "\n",
        "This explains why the choice of algorithm becomes increasingly important as the problem gets larger."
      ]
    },
