      "source": [
        "## 4. Comparing searching algorithms\n",
        "\n",
        "Searching provides a useful way of understanding algorithmic complexity because we have encountered two different approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.1 Linear search: \\(O(n)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Linear search checks each element in turn until it finds the target or reaches the end of the list.\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "def linear_search(numbers, target):\n",
        "\n",
        "    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n",
        "\n",
        "        if numbers[i] == target:\n",
        "            return i\n",
        "\n",
        "    return -1\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "numbers = [12, 8, 35, 19, 42, 7, 51]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Searching for 42 requires five comparisons.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the target is not present, the algorithm must inspect every element before concluding that the value is missing.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Best case:** \\(O(1)\\), if the target is the first element.\n",
        "- **Worst case:** \\(O(n)\\), if the target is last or absent.\n",
        "- **Average case:** \\(O(n)\\), assuming a typical unsorted list.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.2 Binary search: \\(O(\\log n)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Binary search takes a different approach. Rather than checking every element, it repeatedly divides the search space in half.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** Binary search only works correctly when the data is sorted.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "numbers = [3, 7, 12, 19, 25, 35, 42, 51, 60]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we are searching for 42:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Look at the middle element: 25.\n",
        "2. 42 is greater than 25, so discard the lower half.\n",
        "3. Look at the middle of the remaining section: 42.\n",
        "4. The target is found.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each comparison eliminates approximately half of the remaining possibilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "For an input of size \\(n\\), the number of halvings is approximately:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\log_2(n)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "| Number of elements | Approximate halvings |\n",
        "|---:|---:|\n",
        "| 8 | 3 |\n",
        "| 16 | 4 |\n",
        "| 32 | 5 |\n",
        "| 64 | 6 |\n",
        "| 128 | 7 |\n",
        "| 1,024 | 10 |\n",
        "| 1,000,000 | 20 |\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why binary search has logarithmic complexity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def binary_search(numbers, target):\n",
        "    low = 0\n",
        "    high = len(numbers) - 1\n",
        "\n",
        "    while low <= high:\n",
        "        middle = (low + high) // 2\n",
        "\n",
        "        if numbers[middle] == target:\n",
        "            return middle\n",
        "        elif numbers[middle] < target:\n",
        "            low = middle + 1\n",
        "        else:\n",
        "            high = middle - 1\n",
        "\n",
        "    return -1\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "numbers = [3, 7, 12, 19, 25, 35, 42, 51, 60]\n",
        "print(binary_search(numbers, 42))\n",
        "print(binary_search(numbers, 20))"
      ]
    },
