      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Comparing sorting algorithms\n",
        "\n",
        "Different sorting algorithms can produce exactly the same result but require very different amounts of work.\n",
        "\n",
        "We will compare:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Selection sort\n",
        "- Insertion sort\n",
        "- Bubble sort\n",
        "- Merge sort\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.1 Selection sort: \\(O(n^2)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Selection sort repeatedly finds the smallest element in the unsorted part of the list and moves it into the correct position.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "numbers = [5, 3, 8, 1, 4]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The algorithm:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Finds the smallest element (1) and moves it to the first position.\n",
        "2. Finds the smallest remaining element (3) and moves it to the second position.\n",
        "3. Repeats until the list is sorted.\n",
        "\n",
        "The number of comparisons is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "(n-1)+(n-2)+\\ldots+2+1\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "This sum is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = \\frac{n^2-n}{2}\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "For large \\(n\\), the \\(n^2\\) term dominates, so we describe the complexity as \\(O(n^2)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "If we double the input size, the number of comparisons increases approximately fourfold.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.2 Insertion sort: \\(O(n^2)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Insertion sort builds a sorted section of the list, inserting each new element into its correct position.\n",
        "\n",
        "Think about sorting a hand of playing cards:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Pick up the next card.\n",
        "2. Compare it with the cards already sorted.\n",
        "3. Move larger cards to the right.\n",
        "4. Insert the new card into the gap.\n",
        "\n",
        "In the worst case, the list is in reverse order. Every new element must be moved past all the elements that came before it.\n",
        "\n",
        "The number of comparisons or movements is approximately:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "1+2+3+\\ldots+(n-1)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Again, this gives \\(O(n^2)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "However, if the list is already sorted, each new element only needs to be checked against the previous element. Its best-case complexity is therefore \\(O(n)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "This makes insertion sort particularly useful for small or nearly sorted datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.3 Bubble sort: \\(O(n^2)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.\n",
        "\n",
        "After one pass through the list, the largest element has moved to the end. The algorithm repeats until the list is sorted.\n",
        "\n",
        "In the worst case, bubble sort performs approximately \\(n^2\\) operations.\n",
        "\n",
        "An optimised implementation can stop early if no swaps are made during a pass. If the list is already sorted, this gives a best-case complexity of \\(O(n)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.4 Merge sort: \\(O(n \\log n)\\)\n",
        "\n",
        "Merge sort uses a strategy called **divide and conquer**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The algorithm:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Divides the list into two halves.\n",
        "2. Recursively divides each half until individual elements remain.\n",
        "3. Merges the smaller lists back together in sorted order.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are two important observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "- The list is divided approximately \\(\\log_2(n)\\) times.\n",
        "- At each level, merging the lists requires processing approximately \\(n\\) elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "O(n)\\times O(\\log n) = O(n\\log n)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "This grows much more slowly than \\(O(n^2)\\)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 5.5 Comparing the sorting algorithms\n",
        "\n",
        "| Algorithm | Best case | Average case | Worst case |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| Selection sort | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(n^2)\\) |\n",
        "| Insertion sort | \\(O(n)\\) | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(n^2)\\) |\n",
