      "source": [
        "## 7. Best case, worst case and average case\n",
        "\n",
        "The same algorithm can perform differently depending on the input.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Best case:** The input arrangement that allows the algorithm to perform the least work.\n",
        "- **Worst case:** The input arrangement that requires the algorithm to perform the most work.\n",
        "- **Average case:** The expected amount of work across a range of possible inputs, under assumptions about their distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider linear search:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "def linear_search(numbers, target):\n",
        "\n",
        "    for number in numbers:\n",
        "\n",
        "        if number == target:\n",
        "            return True\n",
        "\n",
        "    return False\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the list contains 1,000 elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Situation | Number of comparisons |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| Target is first | 1 |\n",
        "| Target is in the middle | Approximately 500 |\n",
        "| Target is last | 1,000 |\n",
        "| Target is absent | 1,000 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The best case is \\(O(1)\\), but the worst case is \\(O(n)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** Big O does not automatically mean worst case. Big O describes an upper bound on growth. We can use it to describe best-case, average-case or worst-case behaviour.\n",
        "\n",
        "For introductory comparisons, we will often quote the worst-case complexity unless stated otherwise."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 8. Space complexity: How much memory does an algorithm need?\n",
        "\n",
        "Time is not the only computational resource we need to consider.\n",
        "\n",
        "Space complexity describes how the memory requirements of an algorithm change as the input size increases.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider selection sort. It rearranges elements directly within the original list. It only needs a small number of additional variables, such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "min_index = 0\n",
        "temp = numbers[i]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The additional memory requirement does not increase with the size of the list.\n",
        "\n",
        "Its **auxiliary space complexity** is \\(O(1)\\).\n",
        "\n",
        "Now consider merge sort. Merge sort creates additional lists when splitting and merging the input. The additional memory requirement grows with the input size:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "O(n)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "### Comparing time and space\n",
        "\n",
        "| Algorithm | Time complexity (worst case) | Auxiliary space |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|\n",
        "| Selection sort | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(1)\\) |\n",
        "| Insertion sort | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(1)\\) |\n",
        "| Bubble sort | \\(O(n^2)\\) | \\(O(1)\\) |\n",
        "| Merge sort | \\(O(n\\log n)\\) | \\(O(n)\\) |\n",
        "| Linear search | \\(O(n)\\) | \\(O(1)\\) |\n",
        "| Iterative binary search | \\(O(\\log n)\\) | \\(O(1)\\) |\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes we can reduce the time required by an algorithm by using additional memory.\n",
        "\n",
        "Other times, we may need to minimise memory because we are working with an embedded system, a small microcontroller or a resource-constrained robot.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Discussion:** If you were designing software for a small robot with limited memory, would you always choose the algorithm with the lowest time complexity? Why or why not?"
      ]
