        "## 9. Greedy algorithms versus optimal algorithms\n",
        "\n",
        "So far, we have been interested in how efficiently an algorithm performs a task.\n",
        "\n",
        "But there is another important question:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Does an algorithm always find the best possible solution to a problem?**\n",
        "\n",
        "An algorithm can be computationally efficient but still produce a solution that is not the best possible one.\n",
        "\n",
        "This introduces the distinction between greedy algorithms and optimal solutions.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.1 What is a greedy algorithm?\n",
        "\n",
        "A greedy algorithm makes a sequence of decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "At each step, it chooses the option that appears to be the best choice at that particular moment.\n",
        "\n",
        "It does not necessarily consider all possible combinations of decisions or look ahead to determine whether its current choice will lead to the best overall result.\n",
        "\n",
        "The general approach is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Look at the available choices.\n",
        "2. Select the option that appears best locally.\n",
        "3. Commit to that choice.\n",
        "4. Repeat until the problem is solved.\n",
        "\n",
        "The best decision at one particular step does not necessarily lead to the best overall solution."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 9.2 Example: The knapsack problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine you are designing a robot that needs to collect valuable equipment.\n",
        "\n",
        "The robot has a limited carrying capacity of 10 kg.\n",
        "\n",
        "You have three objects available:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Object | Weight | Value | Value per kg |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| A | 6 kg | £12 | £2.00 |\n",
        "| B | 5 kg | £9 | £1.80 |\n",
        "| C | 5 kg | £9 | £1.80 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The robot wants to maximise the total value of the objects it carries without exceeding its 10 kg capacity.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Approach 1: Greedy algorithm\n",
        "\n",
        "A greedy approach might choose the object with the highest value per kilogram first.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Object A has the highest value per kilogram, so select it.\n",
        "2. The robot now has 4 kg of capacity remaining.\n",
        "3. Neither B nor C will fit.\n",
        "4. The algorithm stops.\n",
        "\n",
        "The greedy solution is object A:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Total weight: 6 kg\n",
        "- Total value: £12\n",
        "- Unused capacity: 4 kg\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Approach 2: Find the optimal solution\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of making the locally best decision, consider the possible combinations.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Selected objects | Total weight | Total value |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|\n",
        "| A | 6 kg | £12 |\n",
        "| B | 5 kg | £9 |\n",
        "| C | 5 kg | £9 |\n",
        "| A + B | 11 kg | Not allowed |\n",
        "| A + C | 11 kg | Not allowed |\n",
        "| B + C | 10 kg | £18 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The best solution is to select B and C.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Total weight: 10 kg\n",
        "- Total value: £18\n",
        "\n",
        "The greedy algorithm found a valid solution, but it did not find the optimal solution."
      ]
    },
