        "\n",
        "### 9.3 Why is this important?\n",
        "\n",
        "Greedy algorithms are often attractive because they are relatively simple and computationally efficient.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, they do not always guarantee an optimal solution.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Greedy solution:** Makes the best available decision at each individual step.\n",
        "- **Optimal solution:** Finds the best overall combination of decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "An algorithm that guarantees an optimal solution must account for the interactions between decisions, rather than simply making locally attractive choices.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.4 When does a greedy algorithm work?\n",
        "\n",
        "Greedy algorithms can produce optimal solutions for some problems.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, consider the **fractional knapsack problem**. In this variation, objects can be divided into smaller quantities.\n",
        "\n",
        "The robot could take all of object A and then use its remaining capacity to take part of object B.\n",
        "\n",
        "Choosing objects in order of value per kilogram produces an optimal solution for this version of the problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "The important difference is that objects are divisible.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the indivisible knapsack problem, the same greedy strategy does not guarantee an optimal solution."
