        "## 10. Bringing everything together\n",
        "\n",
        "We have introduced two related but distinct aspects of algorithm design.\n",
        "\n",
        "**1. Computational efficiency**\n",
        "\n",
        "How much time and memory does an algorithm require as the input size increases?\n",
        "\n",
        "**2. Solution quality**\n",
        "\n",
        "Does the algorithm produce a correct solution? Does it guarantee the best possible solution?\n",
        "\n",
        "These are not the same thing.\n",
        "\n",
        "An algorithm can be:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Efficient and optimal.\n",
        "- Efficient but not optimal.\n",
        "- Computationally expensive but optimal.\n",
        "- Computationally expensive and not optimal.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the following examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Algorithm | Main idea | Time complexity | What it guarantees |\n",
        "|---|---|---:|---|\n",
        "| Linear search | Check elements sequentially | \\(O(n)\\) | Finds a target if present |\n",
        "| Binary search | Repeatedly halve the search space | \\(O(\\log n)\\) | Finds a target if present in sorted data |\n",
        "| Selection sort | Repeatedly select the smallest element | \\(O(n^2)\\) | Produces a sorted list |\n",
        "| Insertion sort | Insert elements into a sorted section | \\(O(n^2)\\) worst case | Produces a sorted list |\n",
        "| Merge sort | Divide and merge | \\(O(n\\log n)\\) | Produces a sorted list |\n",
        "| Greedy knapsack | Choose the highest value per unit weight | Depends on implementation | Not always optimal for indivisible objects |\n",
        "\n",
        "Sorting algorithms are designed to produce a correctly sorted result. The difference between them is primarily how efficiently they achieve it.\n",
        "\n",
        "For optimisation problems, the distinction between finding a valid solution and finding the best solution becomes particularly important."
      ]
