        "## 12. Key takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Algorithmic complexity** describes how computational resource requirements grow as the input size increases.\n",
        "- **Big O notation** provides a way to describe the growth of computational work, independently of the particular computer being used.\n",
        "- **Searching:** Linear search is \\(O(n)\\), while binary search is \\(O(\\log n)\\), provided the data is sorted.\n",
        "- **Sorting:** Simple sorting algorithms such as selection sort and insertion sort are generally \\(O(n^2)\\), while merge sort is \\(O(n\\log n)\\).\n",
        "- **Trade-offs:** Algorithms can make different compromises between computational time and memory requirements.\n",
        "- **Greedy versus optimal:** Making the best decision at each step does not necessarily produce the best overall solution.\n",
        "\n",
